From 55dc59248892be169993bb49c025fc0e5ed91c5c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anuj Gupta <anuj20.g@samsung.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 15:48:47 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 14/17] nvme-pci: acquire the dma-buf active reference at
 hardware submission

Wire nvme into map->active, taken and dropped at the actual hardware
boundary rather than at io_uring import time -- the point of the
kref/active split earlier in this series. nvme_iod::flags is widened
from u8 to u16 in the same commit that adds the new IOD_DMABUF_ACTIVE
bit (1U << 8), with an IOD_FLAGS_LAST sentinel and a BUILD_BUG_ON in
nvme_dmabuf_tryget_active() so the field width is checked at compile
time from now on. A too-narrow field silently truncates the |=, which
would make nvme_dmabuf_put_active() a permanent no-op, leak every active
reference, and hang invalidation forever with no diagnostic.

Acquire: first statement of nvme_rq_setup_dmabuf(), before
nvme_pci_dmabuf_sgl_nents() or either setup helper reads any driver DMA
state (map->sgt, map->dma_list, ...). Everything downstream of this
point is already GFP_ATOMIC (dma_pool_alloc() for PRP/SGL descriptors),
so the reference's held window contains no reclaim-capable allocation --
this is the whole point of moving the boundary here instead of leaving
it at import.

Release: last statement of nvme_rq_clean_dmabuf_map(), after
nvme_dmabuf_map_sync_for_cpu() (which still needs a live mapping).

Enforce the boundary with the IOD_DMABUF_ACTIVE flag rather than trust
call-site discipline:

  - nvme_req_dmabuf_map() is now the only accessor to driver DMA state
    outside the unmap callback, and WARN_ON_ONCE()s if the flag isn't
    set. All read sites (both sync helpers, both setup helpers, the SGL
    nents counter) go through it instead of touching bio->bi_dmabuf_map
    directly.
  - nvme_dmabuf_put_active() is a no-op unless the flag is set, so it is
    safe to call unconditionally from any exit path -- double-puts
    become structurally impossible rather than something to audit for.
  - nvme_prep_rq() WARN_ON_ONCE()s the flag is clear *before* its
    `iod->flags = 0` reset. That reset runs on every requeue; without the
    assertion ahead of it, a leaked reference from a prior pass would be
    silently erased before anything could catch it.

nvme_prep_rq()'s failure path (out_free_cmd) skips nvme_unmap_data()
entirely, so nvme_rq_clean_dmabuf_map() does not run for a request that
fails setup. Every such exit inside nvme_rq_setup_dmabuf_map() and
nvme_rq_setup_dmabuf_sgl() now calls nvme_dmabuf_put_active() locally
before returning, so the reference is always balanced regardless of
which path a request takes.

A tryget failure at the funnel (map generation invalidated by exporter
migration) returns NVME_STS_DMABUF_GENERATION_LOST, a documented
BLK_STS_IOERR placeholder -- not BLK_STS_AGAIN, which
include/linux/blk_types.h reserves for RQF_NOWAIT requests whose bio
would block, which this is not. Choosing BLK_STS_IOERR also means
io_uring's transparent reissue never triggers for this failure:
io_rw_should_reissue() and REQ_F_REISSUE are both gated on -EAGAIN (or
-EOPNOTSUPP for the non-IOPOLL completion path), neither of which -EIO
satisfies. This matters because one direct I/O can produce several
requests, and a generation loss between their nvme_queue_rq() calls
would otherwise let io_uring reissue a range that already completed
against the old mapping -- unsafe duplicate I/O under concurrent writers
or on zoned namespaces, not merely redundant. -EIO is not the right
errno for a recoverable, retryable condition; it is the smallest change
for this prototype. A dedicated status (mapping to something like
-ESTALE) is a block/uAPI decision that needs sign-off before this is
upstreamable, and userspace still needs telling that a generation-loss
completion may cover a request that was only partially submitted.

This commit also completes the boundary move begun by the previous one:
dma_buf_io_get_map() / dma_buf_io_create_map() (io_uring's import path)
no longer take a reference on ->active at all -- only nvme's submission
funnel above does, matching the header's ->active contract (acquired
only at hardware submission, never at import). The import-time
percpu_ref_get()/tryget() is removed in the same diff that adds the
submission-time one, so there is no intermediate state where both are
held.

The next commit fixes nvme_prep_rq_batch()'s status handling, which
currently collapses this terminal failure into an indefinite requeue
loop on the nvme_queue_rqs() batch path.

Signed-off-by: Anuj Gupta <anuj20.g@samsung.com>
---
 drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c |   7 +-
 drivers/nvme/host/pci.c      | 168 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 include/linux/dma-buf-io.h   |  38 ++++----
 3 files changed, 178 insertions(+), 35 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c b/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c
index 638bd18e6749..637d9120a673 100644
--- a/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c
+++ b/drivers/dma-buf/dma-buf-io.c
@@ -253,13 +253,10 @@ struct dma_buf_io_map *dma_buf_io_create_map(struct dma_buf_io_ctx *ctx)
 	 * map->refs was kref_init()'d to 1 by dma_buf_io_init_map(), owned
 	 * by ctx (dropped by the release worker once this generation is
 	 * invalidated and unmapped). This get is the calling request's own
-	 * reference, matching the cache-hit path above.
-	 *
-	 * map->active is freshly percpu_ref_init()'d and nothing has had a
-	 * chance to kill it yet -- this cannot fail.
+	 * reference, matching the cache-hit path above. ->active is not
+	 * touched here -- see the ->active comment in dma-buf-io.h.
 	 */
 	kref_get(&map->refs);
-	percpu_ref_get(&map->active);
 	rcu_assign_pointer(ctx->map, map);
 out:
 	dma_resv_unlock(dmabuf->resv);
diff --git a/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c b/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
index 233752a1df77..2cdd732844a3 100644
--- a/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
+++ b/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
@@ -427,6 +427,19 @@ enum nvme_iod_flags {
 
 	/* Metadata using non-coalesced MPTR */
 	IOD_SINGLE_META_SEGMENT	= 1U << 7,
+
+	/*
+	 * This request holds the dma-buf active reference on
+	 * req->bio->bi_dmabuf_map. Set only by a successful
+	 * nvme_dmabuf_tryget_active(), cleared only by
+	 * nvme_dmabuf_put_active(). Driver DMA state on the map may only be
+	 * accessed through nvme_req_dmabuf_map() while this is set -- see
+	 * struct dma_buf_io_map::active in include/linux/dma-buf-io.h.
+	 */
+	IOD_DMABUF_ACTIVE	= 1U << 8,
+
+	/* Sentinel: keep last. Guards the width of nvme_iod::flags. */
+	IOD_FLAGS_LAST,
 };
 
 struct nvme_dma_vec {
@@ -440,7 +453,13 @@ struct nvme_dma_vec {
 struct nvme_iod {
 	struct nvme_request req;
 	struct nvme_command cmd;
-	u8 flags;
+	/*
+	 * Wide enough for every bit in enum nvme_iod_flags. A too-narrow
+	 * type silently truncates |= stores with no compiler diagnostic
+	 * (not even under W=1), so the width is asserted at build time in
+	 * nvme_dmabuf_tryget_active().
+	 */
+	u16 flags;
 	u8 nr_descriptors;
 
 	size_t total_len;
@@ -874,13 +893,104 @@ to_nvme_dmabuf_map(struct dma_buf_io_map *map)
 	return container_of(map, struct nvme_dmabuf_map, base);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Status returned when a request's dma-buf map generation was invalidated
+ * (exporter-driven migration) before nvme_queue_rq() could acquire the
+ * active reference. This is a PROTOTYPE placeholder:
+ *
+ *  - It must never be BLK_STS_AGAIN. include/linux/blk_types.h reserves that
+ *    for RQF_NOWAIT requests whose bio would block; a generation-loss
+ *    rejection satisfies neither condition.
+ *  - It must be terminal, not requeued: nvme_prep_rq_batch() and
+ *    nvme_queue_rqs() must end the request via blk_mq_end_request(), not
+ *    loop it through requeue_list.
+ *  - It must not trigger io_uring's transparent -EAGAIN reissue: one direct
+ *    I/O can produce several requests, and blkdev_dio does not track a
+ *    completed-prefix byte count, so blind reissue would re-issue an
+ *    already-transferred range -- unsafe duplicate I/O, not merely
+ *    redundant, under concurrent writers or zoned namespaces.
+ *
+ * BLK_STS_IOERR is the smallest change for this prototype but is not
+ * semantically accurate: userspace sees -EIO for a recoverable condition.
+ * A dedicated status (mapping to something like -ESTALE) is a block/uAPI
+ * decision that needs sign-off before this is upstreamable.
+ */
+#define NVME_STS_DMABUF_GENERATION_LOST	BLK_STS_IOERR
+
+/*
+ * Acquire the active (hardware DMA) reference on this request's map. Must be
+ * called as the very first thing in the dma-buf submission funnel
+ * (nvme_rq_setup_dmabuf()), before anything reads driver DMA state -- see
+ * nvme_pci_dmabuf_sgl_nents(), which reads map->sgt before either setup
+ * helper runs.
+ *
+ * Returns false if the map has been invalidated (migration in progress);
+ * the caller must fail the request rather than touch any driver map state.
+ */
+static bool nvme_dmabuf_tryget_active(struct request *req)
+{
+	struct nvme_iod *iod = blk_mq_rq_to_pdu(req);
+
+	/*
+	 * A |= of a bit that does not fit in iod->flags truncates silently,
+	 * which would make nvme_dmabuf_put_active() a permanent no-op and
+	 * leak every active reference -- the drain fence would then never
+	 * signal and invalidation would hang forever. Catch it at build time.
+	 */
+	BUILD_BUG_ON((IOD_FLAGS_LAST - 1) > (typeof(iod->flags))~0U);
+
+	if (WARN_ON_ONCE(iod->flags & IOD_DMABUF_ACTIVE))
+		return true;
+
+	if (!dma_buf_io_map_active_tryget(req->bio->bi_dmabuf_map))
+		return false;
+
+	iod->flags |= IOD_DMABUF_ACTIVE;
+	return true;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Release the active reference. Safe to call unconditionally from any exit
+ * path -- a no-op unless IOD_DMABUF_ACTIVE is set, so it cannot double-put.
+ * Must be called exactly once per successful tryget: from normal completion
+ * (nvme_rq_clean_dmabuf_map()) or from local rollback on every setup failure
+ * that does not route through completion (nvme_prep_rq()'s out_free_cmd
+ * skips nvme_unmap_data() entirely, so those paths cannot rely on
+ * nvme_rq_clean_dmabuf_map() running for them).
+ */
+static void nvme_dmabuf_put_active(struct request *req)
+{
+	struct nvme_iod *iod = blk_mq_rq_to_pdu(req);
+
+	if (!(iod->flags & IOD_DMABUF_ACTIVE))
+		return;
+
+	iod->flags &= ~IOD_DMABUF_ACTIVE;
+	dma_buf_io_map_active_put(req->bio->bi_dmabuf_map);
+}
+
+/*
+ * The only accessor for driver DMA state outside the unmap callback (which
+ * runs at active == 0 by definition and uses to_nvme_dmabuf_map() directly).
+ * Asserts the active reference is actually held, so a future patch that
+ * reads map state without first taking the reference fails loudly instead of
+ * racing an in-progress unmap.
+ */
+static inline struct nvme_dmabuf_map *nvme_req_dmabuf_map(struct request *req)
+{
+	struct nvme_iod *iod = blk_mq_rq_to_pdu(req);
+
+	WARN_ON_ONCE(!(iod->flags & IOD_DMABUF_ACTIVE));
+	return to_nvme_dmabuf_map(req->bio->bi_dmabuf_map);
+}
+
 static void nvme_dmabuf_map_sync_for_cpu(struct nvme_dev *nvme_dev,
 					 struct request *req)
 {
 	struct device *dev = nvme_dev->dev;
 	enum dma_data_direction dma_dir;
 	struct bio *bio = req->bio;
-	struct nvme_dmabuf_map *map = to_nvme_dmabuf_map(bio->bi_dmabuf_map);
+	struct nvme_dmabuf_map *map = nvme_req_dmabuf_map(req);
 	dma_addr_t *dma_list = map->dma_list;
 	unsigned offset = bio->bi_iter.bi_offset;
 	unsigned map_idx = offset / NVME_CTRL_PAGE_SIZE;
@@ -902,7 +1012,7 @@ static void nvme_dmabuf_map_sync_for_device(struct nvme_dev *nvme_dev,
 	struct device *dev = nvme_dev->dev;
 	enum dma_data_direction dma_dir;
 	struct bio *bio = req->bio;
-	struct nvme_dmabuf_map *map = to_nvme_dmabuf_map(bio->bi_dmabuf_map);
+	struct nvme_dmabuf_map *map = nvme_req_dmabuf_map(req);
 	dma_addr_t *dma_list = map->dma_list;
 	unsigned offset = bio->bi_iter.bi_offset;
 	unsigned map_idx = offset / NVME_CTRL_PAGE_SIZE;
@@ -918,6 +1028,13 @@ static void nvme_dmabuf_map_sync_for_device(struct nvme_dev *nvme_dev,
 	}
 }
 
+/*
+ * Normal completion funnel. Must run for every request that reached a
+ * successful nvme_dmabuf_tryget_active() and completed submission -- i.e.
+ * every path that does NOT bypass nvme_unmap_data() via nvme_prep_rq()'s
+ * out_free_cmd. The active put is last: nvme_dmabuf_map_sync_for_cpu() still
+ * needs a live, active-held mapping.
+ */
 static void nvme_rq_clean_dmabuf_map(struct nvme_dev *dev,
 				      struct request *req)
 {
@@ -927,6 +1044,8 @@ static void nvme_rq_clean_dmabuf_map(struct nvme_dev *dev,
 
 	if (!(iod->flags & IOD_SINGLE_SEGMENT))
 		nvme_free_descriptors(req);
+
+	nvme_dmabuf_put_active(req);
 }
 
 static blk_status_t nvme_rq_setup_dmabuf_map(struct request *req,
@@ -934,7 +1053,7 @@ static blk_status_t nvme_rq_setup_dmabuf_map(struct request *req,
 {
 	struct nvme_iod *iod = blk_mq_rq_to_pdu(req);
 	struct bio *bio = req->bio;
-	struct nvme_dmabuf_map *map = to_nvme_dmabuf_map(bio->bi_dmabuf_map);
+	struct nvme_dmabuf_map *map = nvme_req_dmabuf_map(req);
 	unsigned bvec_done = bio->bi_iter.bi_offset;
 	unsigned map_idx = bvec_done / NVME_CTRL_PAGE_SIZE;
 	unsigned offset = bvec_done & (NVME_CTRL_PAGE_SIZE - 1);
@@ -964,8 +1083,11 @@ static blk_status_t nvme_rq_setup_dmabuf_map(struct request *req,
 
 	prp_list = dma_pool_alloc(nvme_dma_pool(nvmeq, iod), GFP_ATOMIC,
 			&prp_dma);
-	if (!prp_list)
+	if (!prp_list) {
+		/* Setup failure bypasses nvme_unmap_data(); roll back locally. */
+		nvme_dmabuf_put_active(req);
 		return BLK_STS_RESOURCE;
+	}
 
 	iod->descriptors[iod->nr_descriptors++] = prp_list;
 	prp2_dma = prp_dma;
@@ -997,6 +1119,7 @@ static blk_status_t nvme_rq_setup_dmabuf_map(struct request *req,
 	return BLK_STS_OK;
 free_prps:
 	nvme_free_descriptors(req);
+	nvme_dmabuf_put_active(req);
 	return BLK_STS_RESOURCE;
 }
 
@@ -1309,10 +1432,14 @@ static void nvme_pci_sgl_set_seg(struct nvme_sgl_desc *sge,
 	sge->type = NVME_SGL_FMT_LAST_SEG_DESC << 4;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Called from nvme_rq_setup_dmabuf() after its tryget, so the active
+ * reference is already held here.
+ */
 static unsigned int nvme_pci_dmabuf_sgl_nents(struct request *req)
 {
 	struct bio *bio = req->bio;
-	struct nvme_dmabuf_map *map = to_nvme_dmabuf_map(bio->bi_dmabuf_map);
+	struct nvme_dmabuf_map *map = nvme_req_dmabuf_map(req);
 	struct scatterlist *sg;
 	unsigned long tmp;
 	size_t offset = bio->bi_iter.bi_offset;
@@ -1352,7 +1479,7 @@ static blk_status_t nvme_rq_setup_dmabuf_sgl(struct request *req,
 {
 	struct nvme_iod *iod = blk_mq_rq_to_pdu(req);
 	struct bio *bio = req->bio;
-	struct nvme_dmabuf_map *map = to_nvme_dmabuf_map(bio->bi_dmabuf_map);
+	struct nvme_dmabuf_map *map = nvme_req_dmabuf_map(req);
 	size_t length = blk_rq_payload_bytes(req);
 	struct nvme_sgl_desc *sg_list = &iod->cmd.common.dptr.sgl;
 	bool pooled = entries > 1;
@@ -1363,8 +1490,11 @@ static blk_status_t nvme_rq_setup_dmabuf_sgl(struct request *req,
 	size_t offset = bio->bi_iter.bi_offset;
 	size_t remaining = length;
 
-	if (!entries)
+	if (!entries) {
+		/* Setup failure bypasses nvme_unmap_data(); roll back locally. */
+		nvme_dmabuf_put_active(req);
 		return BLK_STS_IOERR;
+	}
 
 	iod->cmd.common.flags = NVME_CMD_SGL_METABUF;
 	iod->total_len = length;
@@ -1378,8 +1508,10 @@ static blk_status_t nvme_rq_setup_dmabuf_sgl(struct request *req,
 
 		sg_list = dma_pool_alloc(nvme_dma_pool(nvmeq, iod), GFP_ATOMIC,
 					 &sgl_dma);
-		if (!sg_list)
+		if (!sg_list) {
+			nvme_dmabuf_put_active(req);
 			return BLK_STS_RESOURCE;
+		}
 		iod->descriptors[iod->nr_descriptors++] = sg_list;
 	}
 
@@ -1423,6 +1555,7 @@ static blk_status_t nvme_rq_setup_dmabuf_sgl(struct request *req,
 		iod->nr_descriptors--;
 		dma_pool_free(nvme_dma_pool(nvmeq, iod), sg_list, sgl_dma);
 	}
+	nvme_dmabuf_put_active(req);
 	return BLK_STS_IOERR;
 }
 
@@ -1432,6 +1565,15 @@ static blk_status_t nvme_rq_setup_dmabuf(struct request *req,
 	unsigned int entries;
 	size_t avg_seg;
 
+	/*
+	 * Must be the first statement: nothing below may read driver DMA
+	 * state (map->sgt, map->dma_list, ...) before this succeeds. See
+	 * nvme_req_dmabuf_map()'s assertion and the ->active comment in
+	 * include/linux/dma-buf-io.h.
+	 */
+	if (!nvme_dmabuf_tryget_active(req))
+		return NVME_STS_DMABUF_GENERATION_LOST;
+
 	if (use_sgl == SGL_UNSUPPORTED)
 		return nvme_rq_setup_dmabuf_map(req, nvmeq);
 
@@ -1695,6 +1837,14 @@ static blk_status_t nvme_prep_rq(struct request *req)
 	struct nvme_iod *iod = blk_mq_rq_to_pdu(req);
 	blk_status_t ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * A leaked active reference from a prior pass (this request being
+	 * requeued) would otherwise be silently erased by the flags reset
+	 * below, defeating exactly the path -- requeue -- most likely to
+	 * leak one. Must run before the reset.
+	 */
+	WARN_ON_ONCE(iod->flags & IOD_DMABUF_ACTIVE);
+
 	iod->flags = 0;
 	iod->nr_descriptors = 0;
 	iod->total_len = 0;
diff --git a/include/linux/dma-buf-io.h b/include/linux/dma-buf-io.h
index 74ce107aeb2a..43cd9ed7978f 100644
--- a/include/linux/dma-buf-io.h
+++ b/include/linux/dma-buf-io.h
@@ -69,17 +69,20 @@ struct dma_buf_io_map {
 	struct kref			refs;
 
 	/*
-	 * Active reference: gates the DMA mapping. Killed by
-	 * dma_buf_io_drop_map() on invalidation; dev_ops->unmap() only runs
-	 * once every reference taken before the kill has been returned.
+	 * Active reference: gates the DMA mapping. Held only from a
+	 * successful driver-level tryget at hardware submission (e.g.
+	 * nvme_rq_setup_dmabuf() -> nvme_dmabuf_tryget_active()) through the
+	 * matching put at completion or cancellation. This is the only
+	 * reference the drain fence depends on: it is bounded by hardware,
+	 * never by reclaim or task scheduling. dev_ops->unmap() only runs
+	 * once every reference taken before dma_buf_io_drop_map()'s kill has
+	 * been returned.
 	 *
-	 * For now this is acquired and released at exactly the same points
-	 * as ->refs (import and drop) -- it is v5's original single counter,
-	 * split out but not yet retargeted. A later change moves acquisition
-	 * to a driver's hardware-submission funnel, at which point ->refs
-	 * and ->active stop moving together: ->refs is held for a whole
-	 * request's lifetime, ->active only while hardware may be using the
-	 * mapping.
+	 * Deliberately decoupled from ->refs: a request holds ->refs for its
+	 * whole lifetime (including reclaim-capable allocation and
+	 * scheduling), but must not touch ->active -- and therefore must not
+	 * dereference driver DMA state -- until a driver's submission funnel
+	 * has successfully acquired it. See dma_buf_io_map_active_tryget().
 	 */
 	struct percpu_ref		active;
 
@@ -181,11 +184,10 @@ static inline void dma_buf_io_map_active_put(struct dma_buf_io_map *map)
 
 /*
  * dma_buf_io_get_map() / dma_buf_io_map_drop() manage the *software*
- * reference: safe to hold across allocation, reclaim and scheduling. They
- * do not imply the DMA mapping is valid.
- *
- * For now these also take/drop the active reference in lockstep with the
- * software one -- see the ->active comment in struct dma_buf_io_map.
+ * reference only: safe to hold across allocation, reclaim and scheduling.
+ * They do not imply the DMA mapping is valid and do not touch ->active --
+ * a driver's hardware-submission funnel acquires that separately, via
+ * dma_buf_io_map_active_tryget().
  */
 static inline struct dma_buf_io_map *
 dma_buf_io_get_map(struct dma_buf_io_ctx *ctx)
@@ -198,17 +200,11 @@ dma_buf_io_get_map(struct dma_buf_io_ctx *ctx)
 	if (unlikely(!map || !kref_get_unless_zero(&map->refs)))
 		return NULL;
 
-	if (unlikely(!dma_buf_io_map_active_tryget(map))) {
-		kref_put(&map->refs, __dma_buf_io_map_free);
-		return NULL;
-	}
-
 	return map;
 }
 
 static inline void dma_buf_io_map_drop(struct dma_buf_io_map *map)
 {
-	dma_buf_io_map_active_put(map);
 	kref_put(&map->refs, __dma_buf_io_map_free);
 }
 
